@{
    // The published company profile. Written from what this application demonstrably
    // does - the tables it keeps, the screens it shows, the queues it works - rather
    // than from marketing boilerplate, because a profile that promises more than the
    // software delivers is the one thing a visitor can check.
    Layout = "_LandingLayout";
    ViewData["Title"] = "About Verichat";
}

<section class="lnd-section lnd-section--top" id="profile">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-doc__masthead">
            <p class="lnd-kicker">Verichat Quality Projects &middot; Company profile</p>
            <h1 class="lnd-doc__title">The company behind the portal.</h1>
            <p class="lnd-doc__standfirst">
                Verichat supplies medical equipment and consumables to institutions that
                cannot afford to find out about a shortage on the day it matters -
                hospitals and clinics, mine rescue stations, teaching laboratories - and
                services what it installs. The portal you are looking at is not a brochure
                beside the business; it is how the business is run. Every quote, purchase
                order, consignment and service ticket a client has is a row in the same
                system these pages describe.
            </p>

            <dl class="lnd-doc__meta">
                <div>
                    <dt>Head office</dt>
                    <dd>@VerichatContact.Street, @VerichatContact.Suburb, @VerichatContact.City</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>We supply</dt>
                    <dd>Clinical consumables, rehabilitation and mobility equipment, protective wear, laboratory and teaching stock</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>We service</dt>
                    <dd>Installation, calibration and fault response against a scheduled SLA</dd>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <dt>Published</dt>
                    <dd>1 November 2026</dd>
                </div>
            </dl>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-doc__glance">
            <div class="lnd-doc__glance-item">
                <b>Three sectors</b>
                <span>Clinical and hospital, mining and industrial, education and research</span>
            </div>
            <div class="lnd-doc__glance-item">
                <b>One record, start to finish</b>
                <span>The enquiry, the order, the delivery and the service call are the same client file</span>
            </div>
            <div class="lnd-doc__glance-item">
                <b>Answered inside a day</b>
                <span>Every enquiry carries a twenty-four hour response target from the moment it arrives</span>
            </div>
            <div class="lnd-doc__glance-item">
                <b>A person sets the staff side</b>
                <span>Clients sign themselves up to order; a console that sees client data is created by somebody in the company</span>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 01 WHO WE ARE ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="who-we-are">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">01 &mdash; Who we are</p>
            <h2>A supply company that kept losing the plot.</h2>
            <p>
                The problem Verichat set out to solve was not a shortage of stock. It was a
                shortage of answers: which lot is in the carton, has the truck left, who is
                coming to fix the unit that failed last Tuesday, and what did we promise the
                client when we quoted it.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-splits">
            <div class="lnd-split" data-reveal>
                <span class="lnd-split__media">
                    <img src="/images/landing/company-warehouse-aisle.jpg"
                         alt="A stocked warehouse aisle of sealed medical supply cartons on industrial shelving, photographed in cool blue light."
                         loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </span>
                <div class="lnd-split__body">
                    <p class="lnd-split__num">The trade</p>
                    <h3>We move clinical goods, and we say when they arrive.</h3>
                    <p>
                        Verichat holds stock across three kinds of shelf: the consumables a
                        ward gets through in a week, the equipment a department buys once
                        every several years, and the protective wear and teaching stock that
                        institutions reorder by the size run. The first two are the same
                        order to us, because a clinic that has run out of giving sets cannot
                        operate a theatre any more than it can install a wheelchair it never
                        received.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        What we added was the visibility. A client's enquiry is scored and
                        queued rather than left in a shared inbox; a consignment leaves with
                        a tracking number the client can follow without telephoning; and a
                        fault raised against an installed unit is routed by the response time
                        we committed to, not by whoever happens to be free.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="lnd-split" data-reveal>
                <span class="lnd-split__media">
                    <img src="/images/landing/company-clinic-handover.jpg"
                         alt="Two people completing a delivery handover at the door of a clinic, with a sealed transit case between them."
                         loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                </span>
                <div class="lnd-split__body">
                    <p class="lnd-split__num">The platform</p>
                    <h3>The software is the shop floor.</h3>
                    <p>
                        Everything on this site is a view onto the same system our own
                        buyers, drivers and engineers work from - which is why the public
                        pages can be specific. The catalogue is the catalogue. The status a
                        quotation shows is the status the sales desk set. A client who signs
                        in sees their own records because the row-level checks that decide
                        what they may read are the same checks the staff screens obey.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        We build for three departments and one mobile app: the browser
                        portal, and a phone application for the people who are actually in a
                        store cupboard or a mine clinic, ordering and reporting from where
                        the stock is.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<hr class="lnd-hairline" />

<!-- ============ 02 MISSION ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="mission">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">02 &mdash; Mission</p>
            <h2>What we are for, this week.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-statement" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-statement__lead">
                To keep the institutions that treat and teach people supplied, serviced and
                informed - so that a shortage is something they are told about early, and a
                broken unit is something they can see being fixed.
            </p>
            <p class="lnd-statement__body">
                A mission that only describes ambition is not usable, so this one is written
                as the three things the company does every day: quote honestly against stock
                that exists, deliver against the date on the quote, and stay attached to the
                equipment after it is installed. Everything below is one of those three seen
                from further away.
            </p>
            <ul class="lnd-points">
                <li>Quote against real stock and a real lead time, and re-quote rather than miss.</li>
                <li>Tell the client the date and the tracking number, without them asking.</li>
                <li>Own the asset after installation: its warranty, its calibration date and its faults.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 03 VISION ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="vision">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">03 &mdash; Vision</p>
            <h2>Where we are going.</h2>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-statement" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-statement__lead">
                A health institution in this region should be able to run its supply chain
                from a phone in a store cupboard, and see its equipment's whole service
                history before it needs it.
            </p>
            <p class="lnd-statement__body">
                Most of them cannot today. Purchasing lives in a notebook, delivery dates
                live in a phone call and calibration certificates live in a drawer. We are
                working towards the day when that is unusual: when a department can raise a
                request offline, watch it become a consignment, and pull the service record
                on a specific serial number out of the same place it ordered the thing.
            </p>
            <ul class="lnd-points">
                <li>One place per client for enquiry, order, delivery and asset history.</li>
                <li>Stock thresholds that alert before a ward runs out, not after.</li>
                <li>Equipment that carries its own warranty and calibration dates with it.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<hr class="lnd-hairline" />

<!-- ============ 04 VALUES ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="values">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">04 &mdash; Values</p>
            <h2>Six rules that survive contact with a deadline.</h2>
            <p>
                These are written as the decisions they actually settle, because a value
                stated as a virtue is unfalsifiable. Each one below can be checked in the
                software or in a contract.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-tiles" data-reveal>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">01</span>
                <h3>Traceable, or it does not move</h3>
                <p>
                    Consumables carry a lot, equipment carries a serial number, and both are
                    recorded against the client they went to. A recall is a query, not a
                    phone tree.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">02</span>
                <h3>The date we give is the date we work to</h3>
                <p>
                    Quotations carry lead times, enquiries carry a twenty-four hour response
                    target and tickets carry an SLA. Blowing one is written down and
                    escalated, not explained away in a phone call.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">03</span>
                <h3>One record, not four</h3>
                <p>
                    Nothing is re-keyed between the enquiry, the order, the delivery and the
                    service call. The cost of an out-of-date spreadsheet is a client who was
                    promised something the file never said.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">04</span>
                <h3>Access is a decision</h3>
                <p>
                    A client account opens its own portal and nothing else; a staff console is
                    created by an administrator, one named person at a time. Roles are real sets
                    of permissions, and the screens you are not allowed to open are not offered
                    to you in the menu.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">05</span>
                <h3>Say what we hold</h3>
                <p>
                    The <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> names the columns, the reasons
                    and the roles that can read them. There are no trackers on these pages and
                    no advertising networks, so nothing about you is sold to make them cheaper
                    to run.
                </p>
            </article>
            <article class="lnd-tile">
                <span class="lnd-tile__num">06</span>
                <h3>Somebody owns the answer</h3>
                <p>
                    Every screen and every commitment belongs to a named function in the
                    company. If a delivery is late or a unit is overdue for calibration, there
                    is a person whose queue it is already in.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-callout" data-reveal>
            <p>
                <strong>What this page deliberately does not claim.</strong> No turnover, no
                headcount, no number of institutions served and no accreditation or
                certification is stated here, because none of it is held in this system and a
                figure we cannot show you the record for is not a figure we publish. Ask for
                them and we will answer with documents rather than adjectives.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<hr class="lnd-hairline" />

<!-- ============ 05 PROJECTS ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="projects">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">05 &mdash; What we have built</p>
            <h2>Six pieces of work, all in use.</h2>
            <p>
                These are not case studies. They are the parts of the platform the company
                runs today, listed because a profile should be checkable - each one is a
                screen a client or a Verichat employee opens.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-ledger" data-reveal>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">01</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>A ranked enquiry queue</h3>
                    <p>
                        Incoming enquiries are scored on sector, contract status, estimated
                        value and stated urgency, so the next call to make is already at the
                        top of the list. A shared inbox ranks whoever shouts loudest; this
                        ranks the work.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">02</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Quotations a client can answer</h3>
                    <p>
                        A quote leaves as a branded PDF and stays live in the portal, where a
                        client accepts or declines it. The answer arrives as a status on the
                        same record, not as a reply that somebody has to file.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">03</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Warehouse alerts that become purchase orders</h3>
                    <p>
                        Stock below its reorder point raises an alert to procurement, and
                        accepting that alert writes a real order against a named supplier.
                        The alert is the reason a consignment exists.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">04</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Deliveries the client can follow</h3>
                    <p>
                        Every consignment carries a tracking reference and a status history,
                        readable by the client without asking us. Where it is and when it
                        arrives are the same two answers for both sides.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">05</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>An SLA ticket desk attached to an asset register</h3>
                    <p>
                        Faults become tickets, tickets carry a deadline, and escalation is a
                        recorded event rather than an apology. Warranty and calibration dates
                        belong to the asset, so a unit due for service shows up before the
                        department notices.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div>
                <span class="lnd-ledger__num">06</span>
                <div>
                    <h3>Two doors onto the same account</h3>
                    <p>
                        The browser portal and a mobile application for clients and students,
                        which queues an order when there is no signal and sends it when there
                        is. Both are gated by the same permission rules, so
                        neither can see more than the other.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 06 SECTORS ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="sectors-we-serve">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">06 &mdash; Where the work goes</p>
            <h2>Three sectors, three different urgencies.</h2>
            <p>
                The same platform serves all three. What changes is the vocabulary, the
                reorder rhythm and who is allowed to see what.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-sectors">
            @{ var sectorIndex = 0; }
            @foreach (var sector in LandingContent.Sectors)
            {
                sectorIndex++;
                <article class="lnd-sector" data-reveal data-reveal-delay="@(sectorIndex * 110)">
                    <img src="@sector.Image" alt="@sector.Alt" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
                    <span class="lnd-sector__veil"></span>
                    <div class="lnd-sector__body">
                        <p class="lnd-sector__num">0@(sectorIndex)</p>
                        <h3>@sector.Name</h3>
                        <p>@sector.Body</p>
                    </div>
                </article>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 07 WHO OWNS WHAT ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="who-owns-what">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">07 &mdash; Who owns each part</p>
            <h2>A function behind every screen.</h2>
            <p>
                This page is published, so it names the roles accountable for each part of
                the service rather than the people currently holding them.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="lnd-team__grid" data-reveal>
            @foreach (var person in LandingContent.Team)
            {
                <article class="lnd-person">
                    <div class="lnd-person__mono" aria-hidden="true">@person.Initial</div>
                    <h3>@person.Name</h3>
                    <p class="lnd-person__role">@person.Role</p>
                    <p>@person.Focus</p>
                </article>
            }
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 08 HOW TO FIND US ============ -->
<section class="lnd-section" id="find-us">
    <div class="lnd-wrap">
        <div class="lnd-head" data-reveal>
            <p class="lnd-kicker">08 &mdash; How to find us</p>
            <h2>A physical office, with a phone number that rings in it.</h2>
            <p>
                The details below are the ones printed on a quotation, and they are the same
                on the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>, the
                <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> and the services page - held in one
                place in the code so that four pages cannot disagree about a phone number.
            </p>
        </div>

        @await Html.PartialAsync("_PublicContact")

        <div class="lnd-linkrow" data-reveal>
            <a class="lnd-btn lnd-btn--ghost" href="/services">What we do</a>
            <a class="lnd-btn lnd-btn--ghost" href="/contact">Contact us</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ CLOSING ============ -->
<section class="lnd-cta">
    <div class="lnd-wrap" data-reveal>
        <p class="lnd-eyebrow">Verichat Quality Projects</p>
        <h2>The rest is a conversation about your shelf.</h2>
        <p>
            Tell us what a ward, a rescue station or a laboratory gets through, and we will
            quote the supply and the service together.
        </p>
        <div class="lnd-cta__actions">
            <a class="lnd-btn lnd-btn--solid" href="/contact">Send an enquiry &rarr;</a>
            <a class="lnd-btn lnd-btn--ghost" href="/services">What we do</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
